WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.467
Let's walk through a simple example.

2
00:00:01.533 --> 00:00:03.527
Imagine you have a red ten in one column,

3
00:00:03.547 --> 00:00:06.200
and a black nine is covering a hidden card in another.

4
00:00:06.267 --> 00:00:08.400
You can move the black nine onto the red ten.

5
00:00:08.467 --> 00:00:10.000
Doing so reveals a new card.

6
00:00:10.067 --> 00:00:11.567
Suppose that card is a red eight.

7
00:00:11.633 --> 00:00:14.167
You now have new information and potentially another move.

8
00:00:14.233 --> 00:00:17.000
Maybe that red eight can be placed on a black nine somewhere else.

9
00:00:17.067 --> 00:00:19.567
If you can do that, you may reveal another hidden card.

10
00:00:19.633 --> 00:00:20.833
This is how moves connect.

11
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:22.933
One move creates an opportunity for another.

12
00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:26.533
But sometimes the best move is not the one that immediately reveals a card.

13
00:00:26.600 --> 00:00:30.167
Maybe moving a card to a foundation removes something you need in the tableau.

14
00:00:30.233 --> 00:00:33.000
Maybe an empty column should be saved for a specific King.

15
00:00:33.067 --> 00:00:37.000
Maybe a card in the waste becomes useful only after you reorganize another column.

16
00:00:37.067 --> 00:00:40.333
So instead of asking, "What can I move right now?", start asking:

17
00:00:40.400 --> 00:00:42.167
"What does this move allow me to do next?"

18
00:00:42.233 --> 00:00:44.433
That one question can change how you approach the game.

19
00:00:44.500 --> 00:00:46.100
Let's also look at a common mistake.

20
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:48.667
A player sees a legal move and immediately makes it.

21
00:00:48.733 --> 00:00:51.133
But a legal move is not necessarily a useful move.

22
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:53.136
Sometimes two different moves are available,

23
00:00:53.156 --> 00:00:55.567
and one gives you much more flexibility than the other.

24
00:00:55.633 --> 00:00:59.233
For example, one move might simply rearrange two visible cards.

25
00:00:59.300 --> 00:01:01.789
Another might expose a hidden card, open a column,

26
00:01:01.809 --> 00:01:03.867
or make an important sequence accessible.

27
00:01:03.933 --> 00:01:06.700
When you have a choice, consider the consequences before committing.

28
00:01:06.767 --> 00:01:08.933
And remember that foundation takebacks exist.

29
00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:12.277
If you discover that a card you moved to a foundation is needed in the tableau,

30
00:01:12.297 --> 00:01:14.133
check whether you can legally bring it back.

31
00:01:14.200 --> 00:01:17.433
You do not need to treat every foundation move as irreversible.

32
00:01:17.500 --> 00:01:21.021
The goal is to keep the board flexible while steadily progressing toward the

33
00:01:21.041 --> 00:01:21.600
foundations.

34
00:01:23.000 --> 00:01:24.500
So, what does it actually mean to win?

35
00:01:24.567 --> 00:01:27.833
In standard Klondike Solitaire, the objective is straightforward.

36
00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.733
Move all fifty-two cards into the four foundations.

37
00:01:30.800 --> 00:01:33.100
Every suit must be built from Ace through King.

38
00:01:33.167 --> 00:01:36.700
Once all four foundations are complete, the Solitaire puzzle is solved.

39
00:01:36.767 --> 00:01:39.367
But GRYND adds another dimension through Solitaire Duel.

40
00:01:39.433 --> 00:01:43.033
In Duel, two players compete using the same guaranteed-solvable deal.

41
00:01:43.100 --> 00:01:45.600
Both players are trying to complete the same Solitaire puzzle.

42
00:01:45.667 --> 00:01:49.333
The first player to move all fifty-two cards into the foundations wins.

43
00:01:49.400 --> 00:01:51.433
But what happens if neither player completes the game?

44
00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.133
That's where the progress ladder comes in.

45
00:01:53.200 --> 00:01:56.000
Progress is compared through the game's defined progress system.

46
00:01:56.067 --> 00:02:00.467
Foundation progress is prioritized first, followed by revealed tableau progress.

47
00:02:00.533 --> 00:02:04.116
This means that making progress is important even when you haven't completed the

48
00:02:04.136 --> 00:02:04.767
entire puzzle.

49
00:02:04.833 --> 00:02:08.324
You should still focus on building foundations and revealing hidden cards,

50
00:02:08.344 --> 00:02:10.967
because those actions contribute to meaningful progress.

51
00:02:11.033 --> 00:02:12.233
There is no match clock.

52
00:02:12.300 --> 00:02:12.674
However,

53
00:02:12.694 --> 00:02:16.633
a player who remains inactive for twenty minutes can forfeit through inactivity.

54
00:02:16.700 --> 00:02:20.145
The important difference is that Duel isn't simply about finishing Solitaire

55
00:02:20.165 --> 00:02:20.667
eventually.

56
00:02:20.733 --> 00:02:24.385
It's about making efficient decisions and progressing toward the objective faster

57
00:02:24.405 --> 00:02:25.267
than your opponent.

58
00:02:25.333 --> 00:02:27.733
A move that reveals a hidden card can be valuable.

59
00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:30.500
A move that opens a column can create new opportunities.

60
00:02:30.567 --> 00:02:34.000
And a move that helps complete a foundation can bring you closer to victory.

61
00:02:34.067 --> 00:02:36.300
The same fundamental Solitaire rules still apply.

62
00:02:36.367 --> 00:02:40.506
You still need descending ranks, alternating colors, valid foundation sequences,

63
00:02:40.526 --> 00:02:42.033
and careful use of the stock.

64
00:02:42.100 --> 00:02:45.100
But now, every decision also has a competitive dimension.

65
00:02:45.167 --> 00:02:46.700
GRYND includes several ways to play:

66
00:02:46.767 --> 00:02:48.500
Solo, where you can play on your own.

67
00:02:48.567 --> 00:02:50.933
Daily Challenge, where you can take on the daily puzzle.

68
00:02:51.000 --> 00:02:54.433
Practice vs AI, where you can practice against an AI opponent.

69
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:57.533
Ranked PvP, for competitive Solitaire Duel matches.

70
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:01.055
Whether you're learning the game or trying to improve your competitive performance,

71
00:03:01.075 --> 00:03:02.633
the underlying skills remain the same.

72
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:03.667
Understand the board.

73
00:03:03.733 --> 00:03:04.833
Reveal hidden cards.

74
00:03:04.900 --> 00:03:06.167
Preserve useful options.

75
00:03:06.233 --> 00:03:08.200
And think about what each move makes possible.

76
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:12.033
Before we finish, here are a few habits worth remembering.

77
00:03:12.100 --> 00:03:13.333
First, don't rush.

78
00:03:13.400 --> 00:03:16.733
Even when a move looks obvious, take a moment to consider what it changes.

79
00:03:16.800 --> 00:03:19.000
Second, prioritize useful information.

80
00:03:19.067 --> 00:03:21.747
Revealing a hidden card can open up new possibilities,

81
00:03:21.767 --> 00:03:25.667
so look for moves that uncover cards without sacrificing too much flexibility.

82
00:03:25.733 --> 00:03:27.867
Third, use empty columns carefully.

83
00:03:27.933 --> 00:03:29.176
Only Kings can fill them,

84
00:03:29.196 --> 00:03:32.900
and an empty column can be extremely useful when reorganizing the tableau.

85
00:03:32.967 --> 00:03:35.000
Fourth, don't forget the stock and waste.

86
00:03:35.067 --> 00:03:36.663
The top waste card may be useful,

87
00:03:36.683 --> 00:03:40.729
and unlimited redeals give you another opportunity to access cards you haven't been

88
00:03:40.749 --> 00:03:41.533
able to use yet.

89
00:03:41.600 --> 00:03:44.867
Fifth, understand that foundation moves can sometimes be reversed.

90
00:03:44.933 --> 00:03:46.509
If a card is needed in the tableau,

91
00:03:46.529 --> 00:03:48.900
a legal takeback can help you reorganize your board.

92
00:03:48.967 --> 00:03:51.567
And finally, learn to think one or two moves ahead.

93
00:03:51.633 --> 00:03:55.933
Instead of making the first legal move you see, ask yourself what that move unlocks.

94
00:03:56.000 --> 00:03:59.100
That's how you start recognizing patterns and making better decisions.

95
00:03:59.167 --> 00:04:00.833
The best way to improve is to play.

96
00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:03.238
Try a few games, experiment with different moves,

97
00:04:03.258 --> 00:04:06.000
and pay attention to the decisions that lead to progress.

98
00:04:06.067 --> 00:04:08.633
If you're new to Solitaire, start with Solo or Practice.

99
00:04:08.700 --> 00:04:12.133
When you're ready for more competition, explore the Duel experience on GRYND.

100
00:04:12.200 --> 00:04:16.133
And remember: the rules are simple, but mastering the decisions takes practice.

101
00:04:16.200 --> 00:04:18.433
Thanks for watching, and welcome to GRYND Academy.
